<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xenophon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 430–c. 354 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xenophon was an Athenian soldier, historian and philosopher of the late fifth and fourth centuries BCE, a pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a>. He served with the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> hired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-younger">Cyrus the Younger</a> in 401 BCE and led their retreat after Cyrus's death. His account, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabasis">Anabasis</a>, demonstrated that a small Greek force could cross the Persian heartland with impunity, exposing the empire's vulnerability.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xenophon.jpg" alt="Xenophon" width="278" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Xenophon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Xenophon was born in the Athenian deme of Erchia, the son of Gryllus, who belonged to an aristocratic family. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> was fought through his childhood and youth. Like many wealthy young men of the time he associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a>, and he probably served in the Athenian cavalry. He seems to have stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">Thirty Tyrants</a> and to have fought the democratic insurgents in the civil war of 404 to 403 BCE. Xenophon was invited by Proxenus of Beotia to join the army that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-younger">Cyrus the Younger</a> was raising against the Persian satrap Tissaphernes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Cyrus gathered a large army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, and the Greeks did not know at first that it was meant against King Artaxerxes II. After Cyrus was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cunaxa">Battle of Cunaxa</a>, the Spartan general Clearchus and four other generals were seized and executed on the invitation of Tissaphernes. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-thousand">Ten Thousand</a>, then elected new leaders, among them Xenophon.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabasis">Anabasis</a> he describes the retreat. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> were harassed by Persian missile cavalry, fought the Carduchians in the mountains, forced a crossing of the Centrites River and marched through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in winter without suitable clothing. They reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trapezus</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast and fought the Colchians before making their way west. The military historian Theodore Ayrault Dodge called Xenophon the father of the system of retreat.</p><p>Afterwards Xenophon served with Spartan commanders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and joined the campaign of King Agesilaus. Athens banished him for fighting on the Spartan side at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-coronea-394-bc">Battle of Coronea</a> in 394 BCE. He received an estate at Scillus, where he lived for twenty-three years until the Elians confiscated it after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>'s defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Leuctra</a> in 371 BCE. According to Diogenes Laertius he then moved to Corinth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Xenophon's whole body of work survives. It includes the Hellenica, which continues <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a>' account of the Peloponnesian War to 362 BCE and is partial to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, the Cyropaedia, the biography Agesilaus, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-lacedaemonians">Constitution of the Lacedaemonians</a>, and short treatises on horsemanship, hunting with dogs and the finances of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. His Socratic works are the Memorabilia, Apology, Symposium and Oeconomicus; apart from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s, they are the only surviving Socratic dialogues.</p><p>The Cyropaedia gives a fictional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> the qualities of an ideal ruler, and it differs from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> on several points of his career. Some scholars read it as a warning that empires hold together only through a ruler of exceptional ability, and as a criticism of Athenian and Spartan ambitions for empire.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Xenophon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The strategic damage was especially great: <b>Xenophon</b>'s Anabasis showed that a relatively small Greek force could march through the Persian heartland and escape, and so that the empire was vulnerable to any sufficiently determined adversary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Odrysian rulers also hired Greek soldiers: in 400–399 BCE Seuthes II employed the survivors of <b>Xenophon</b>’s Ten Thousand to subdue tribes near the Propontis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/odrysian-kingdom">Odrysian Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ancient writers from <b>Xenophon</b>, in his Constitution of the Lacedaemonians, to Plato, Aristotle and Plutarch discussed Sparta as a model, or a warning, of a state that shaped its citizens through law and education.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Armenians were known long before their language was written: they are named in the 6th-century BCE Behistun Inscription and in <b>Xenophon</b>’s Anabasis of the 4th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/language/armenian">Armenian · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Xenophon</b> favoured a moderate oligarchy, and Polybius made oligarchy the degenerate successor of aristocracy in his cycle of constitutions.</blockquote><a href="/government/oligarchy">Oligarchy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Xenophon</b>’s Hiero imagined the tyrant of Syracuse confessing his unhappiness to the poet Simonides, and Aristotle’s Politics analysed how tyrannies arose, how they were preserved and why they fell.</blockquote><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Erchia, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Corinth, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.97,23.92&amp;year=-381" aria-label="Erchia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5146 799 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Xenophon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5596 1069h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5596 1069h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.97,23.92&amp;year=-381">What was here in 381 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…it is not numbers or strength that gives victory in war; but, heaven helping them, to one or other of two combatants it is given to dash with stouter hearts to meet the foe…&#8221;</p><footer>Anabasis, book 3 &#183; tr. H. G. Dakyns, 1890 &#183; his own speech to the officers of the Ten Thousand, as he gives it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1170/pg1170-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;We have had occasion before now to reflect how often democracies have been overthrown by the desire for some other type of government, how often monarchies and oligarchies have been swept away by movements of the people…&#8221;</p><footer>Cyropaedia, book 1 &#183; tr. H. G. Dakyns, 1914 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2085/pg2085-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The thing is to get them to turn their thoughts to what they mean to do, instead of to what they are likely to suffer.&#8221;</p><footer>Anabasis, book 3 &#183; tr. H. G. Dakyns, 1890 &#183; his own advice to the officers of the Ten Thousand after their generals were seized, as he gives it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1170/pg1170-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
